WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.340
SPEAKER: Say you've
got a vision,

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:04.980
the next big IoT device that
will revolutionize the way

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.470
people interact with all of
their household appliances.

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:09.000
With your new IoT device--

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.426
and let's call it Smart Fridge--

00:00:10.426 --> 00:00:13.050
connected refrigerators will be
able to predict when foods will

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:16.320
go stale and even automatically
reorder those items directly

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.450
from a grocer to keep
the stock up to date.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:19.470
It's all flawless.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:22.280
You know exactly what it
will do and who needs it.

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:24.780
But once you get that product
in the hands of millions-- no,

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:27.690
billions-- how will you handle
the vast amounts of data that's

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:29.700
going to come pouring in?

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.460
Have you figured out how to
collect, process, and analyze

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.000
all of your IoT data?

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.050
You may want to consider
using Cloud IoT Core.

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:39.210
Cloud IoT Core is a
fully managed service

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:42.300
that allows you to easily
and securely connect, manage,

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:46.050
and ingest data from millions
of globally dispersed devices.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:47.080
How does it work?

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:49.230
Well, let's head
over to Qwiklabs.

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:51.960
In the lab, building an IoT
analytics pipeline on Google

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.610
Cloud Platform, you'll
learn how to ingest

00:00:54.610 --> 00:00:57.760
the stream of information from
Cloud IoT Core using Cloud

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:01.570
Pub/Sub, process all that
data using Cloud Dataflow,

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:05.355
connect and manage MQTT-based
devices using Cloud IoT Core.

00:01:05.355 --> 00:01:06.730
And for the purposes
of this lab,

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:08.310
we'll be using
simulated devices.

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:11.310
Lastly, you'll be able to
analyze all of that IoT data

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:13.020
using Google BigQuery.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:14.550
And you can use
your IoT data stream

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:17.042
for things like advanced
analytics, visualizations,

00:01:17.042 --> 00:01:18.500
and even machine
learning, which is

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.416
gonna help you improve
operational efficiency,

00:01:20.416 --> 00:01:23.040
anticipate problems, and
build rich models that

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.900
will help you better describe
and optimize your business.

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:27.530
The lab will take you less
than an hour to complete,

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:28.904
and it'll give
you an opportunity

00:01:28.904 --> 00:01:30.590
to see if Cloud IoT
Core is going to be

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:32.670
a good fit for Smart Fridge.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:35.340
Qwiklabs is an online,
hands-on lab library

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:38.010
with more than 150 labs
in every Cloud topic,

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:40.440
ranging from introductory
to guru level.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:42.960
Each lab will help you learn
a new skill in about an hour

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:44.059
using Google Cloud.

00:01:44.059 --> 00:01:45.600
When you're ready,
use the link below

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.600
to take the lab that we
just went through today.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:50.160
And if you're ready to sign
up for GCP, look for that link

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.520
below, and you can apply a
$300 credit to your account.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.800
Today I'll be responding
to a request from Dan Pham

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:57.480
from one of our
previous episodes.

